A content site rarely needs JavaScript on its public pages. Mortar renders on the server (brick loaders included), so you can send those pages as plain HTML and hydrate only the admin.

`createRender` from `@mortar-kit/core/render` does it. Use it as your server entry, and put `<ClientScripts />` in your document's head in place of the hydration script and client entry:

```tsx
// src/entry-server.tsx
import { createRender } from '@mortar-kit/core/render';
import manifest from 'virtual:solid-manifest';
import { Root } from './Root.tsx';

export const render = createRender({ manifest, app: () => <Root /> });
```

```tsx
// src/entry-client.tsx
import { hydrate } from '@solidjs/web';
import { Root } from './Root.tsx';

hydrate(() => <Root />, document);
```

```tsx
// src/Document.tsx (Root renders it around your App)
import { ClientScripts } from '@mortar-kit/core';

export default function Document(props) {
  return (
    <html lang="en">
      <head>
        <meta charset="utf-8" />
        <ClientScripts />
      </head>
      <body>{props.children}</body>
    </html>
  );
}
```

`hydrate` picks the pages that load JavaScript. The default is `['/admin']`, so the editor and its preview hydrate. It also takes path prefixes (matched by whole segments, in any case), `true`, `false`, or a function of the path. On every other page:

- the render runs under `<NoHydration>` with `noScripts`, so there are no hydration keys and no hydration data;
- `<ClientScripts>` renders nothing, so there's no client entry (unless the page has [islands](#islands));
- the manifest gives stylesheets only, so the browser doesn't preload any JavaScript either;
- the page is always sent as one settled document, since streamed content needs a script.

When the plugin generates your entries, every page hydrates. Authoring the two entries above is what switches that off. `createRender` puts the request's CSP nonce on the scripts of hydrating pages.

The result: the example site's home page is about 1.3 kB of HTML plus two stylesheets, and no scripts.

## Islands

A brick that needs the browser on a public page (a form that answers in place, a gallery, a calculator) says so:

```tsx
export const contactForm = defineBrick({
  name: 'contact-form',
  interactive: true,
  // schema, action, component as usual
});
```

The page knows its bricks before it's sent, so nothing else has to change on the page. When a page without JavaScript shows an interactive brick:

- the brick renders in an **island**: an element around its own `<Hydration>` zone, carrying its data, what its `load` returned and its latest form result as JSON;
- `createRender` adds Solid's hydration bootstrap and your client entry to that page only. Pages without an interactive brick stay script-free;
- in the browser, only the islands hydrate. The rest of the page is never touched.

Your client entry decides between the admin and islands. Importing each side lazily keeps the admin out of public pages:

```tsx
// src/entry-client.tsx
import { hasIslands } from '@mortar-kit/core';
import { hydrate } from '@solidjs/web';

if (hasIslands()) {
  void import('./islands.ts').then((islands) => islands.start());
} else {
  void import('./Root.tsx').then(({ Root }) => hydrate(() => <Root />, document));
}
```

```ts
// src/islands.ts
import { hydrateIslands, islandActions } from '@mortar-kit/core';
import { brickActions } from './actions.ts';
import { bricks } from './bricks/index.ts';
import { runBrickAction } from './server/content.ts';

export function start() {
  hydrateIslands({ bricks, actions: islandActions({ action: brickActions.action, run: runBrickAction }) });
}
```

`islandActions` is what lets an island's `<ActionForm>` post without the router: it calls your action's server function in the background and hands the brick each `pending` and result, so only the brick updates (see [Handling forms](/docs/forms)). Without JavaScript, the same form still posts plainly.

In the admin, interactive bricks render like any other brick, since the whole page hydrates there.

Keep in mind:

- An island's data is in the page's HTML. Mark fields only the server needs `private: true` (see [Fields](/docs/fields)), and they're left out. The contact form's "Send to" address is one.
- `load` runs on the server only. The island gets its result and doesn't load again in the browser, so return plain JSON.
- Do async work in `load`, not in the component: an island has no hydration data of its own to wait for.
